{{Quote|It's too far to walk, but I've got something else that'll get you there fast.|[[Nash Durango]], to the [[Jedi Initiate]]s|<ref name="Yoda's Mission">{{YJA|Yoda's Mission}}</ref>}}
'''Speeder bikes''', also known as '''jumpspeeders''', were open-air [[repulsorlift]] vehicles that emphasized speed and maneuverability over stability. A particularly powerful speeder bike was called a [[swoop]].

The [[Jedi]] used the [[Undicur-class jumpspeeder|''Undicur''-class jumpspeeder]], which proved popular with civilians.<ref name="Head2Head">''[[Star Wars Rebels: Head to Head]]''</ref> Another civilian speeder bike was the [[Joben T-85 speeder bike]], used by the Jedi [[Kanan Jarrus]].<ref name="Kanan's Speeder Bike">{{Databank|kanans-speeder-bike|Kanan's Speeder Bike}}</ref>

The [[Clone Wars]] saw the development of the [[CK-6 swoop bike]]s for use in cold climates,<ref name="Databank Freeco">{{Databank|freeco-bike|Freeco Bike}}</ref> as well as [[combat speeder]]s designed for the [[Confederacy of Independent Systems|Separatist]] warlord [[General]] [[Grievous]].<ref name="SWE entry">'{{SWE|technology|general-grievous-speeder|General Grievous' Speeder}}</ref> [[Hondo Ohnaka]]'s [[Ohnaka Gang|gang]] rode [[Starhawk speeder bike]]s.<ref name="Dooku Captured">{{TCW|Dooku Captured}}</ref>

The [[Galactic Empire|Imperial]] speeder bike lineage could be traced to the [[BARC speeder]]s used during the Clone Wars.<ref name="Episode III">{{Film|III}}</ref> Imperial forces used the [[614-AvA speeder bike]] until it was phased out by the [[74-Z speeder bike|74-Z]].<ref name="RotJ">{{Film|VI}}</ref>

Speeder bikes were sometimes a target for theft. [[Merei Spanjaf]], a teenage girl living on [[Lothal]], owned a [[Merei Spanjaf's jumpspeeder|jumpspeeder]] that could fit into a [[speeder van]]. She normally wore a helmet and [[goggles]] when riding it.<ref name="justice">''[[Servants of the Empire: Imperial Justice]]''</ref>

On [[At Attin]] during the [[New Republic Era]], [[Fern]] and [[KB]] owned a [[Fern and KB's hoverbike|modified two-being]] [[hoverbike]], while [[Wim]] and [[Neel]] owned one-being hoverbikes.

[[Jace Rucklin]] had two speeder bikes, one [[Rucklin's speederbike (orange)|orange]] and [[Rucklin's speederbike (blue)|one blue]] when he befriend [[Kazuda Xiono]]. Unknown to Xiono, his blue bike was purposely sabotage.<ref name="Fuel for the Fire">{{Resistance|Fuel for the Fire}}</ref> The [[Warbird gang]] also use the same speeder bike model, during their raid on the ''[[Colossus]]''<ref name="Synara's Score">{{Resistance|Synara's Score}}</ref> and when they kidnapped [[Torra Doza]].<ref name="The Doza Dilemma">{{Resistance|The Doza Dilemma}}</ref> On [[Varkana]], [[Synara San]] stole the same speeder model when she chase after [[Ax Tagrin]]. [[Norath Kev]] and Xiono escape on San's speeder bike.<ref name="The Missing Agent">{{Resistance|The Missing Agent}}</ref>

{{Veh-stub}}

==Behind the scenes==
The ''Undicur''-class jumpspeeder, 614-AvA speeder bike, and Starhawk speeder bike were all based on a vehicle concept by [[Joe Johnston]]<ref name="SW-Repurposed Star Wars Technology - Back from the Drawing Board, Part 2">{{SW|url=news/back-from-the-drawing-board-part-2-repurposed-star-wars-technology|text=Repurposed ''Star Wars'' Technology - Back from the Drawing Board, Part 2|nolive=1}}</ref> for [[Star Wars: Episode VI Return of the Jedi|''Return of the Jedi'']].

[[Jace Rucklin|Rucklin]]'s speederbikes in [[Fuel for the Fire]] were also made from early concept arts of ''Return of the Jedi''.<ref name="SW-Designing Star Wars: Star Wars Resistance">{{SW|url=news/designing-star-wars-resistance|text=Designing ''Star Wars'': ''Star Wars Resistance''}}</ref>

